doctors in Pennsylvania are racing to  solve a deadly medical mystery they're  trying to find the source of a bacterial  infection  apparently linked to the deaths of three  babies inside a hospital our  investigative correspondent and ordinary  is looking into this Anna this is such a  scary story what is the germ and who is  at risk  so it's called Pseudomonas it's a very  common waterborne bacterium that is  often harmless but in this hospital's  neonatal intensive care unit it became  deadly hospital officials say eight  babies developed a Pseudomonas infection  at Geisinger Medical Center in Danville  that's about 50 miles north of  Harrisburg three of the infants died the  other four are doing well another is  still being treated now doctors say the  babies were vulnerable to the infection  because they were born prematurely with  weak immune systems now the hospital is  trying to make sure other patients are  safe we made sure that we increase the  chlorination of the water we've put some  special filters on the taps we have done  extra cleaning even though we didn't  find the organism on the surfaces the  hospital issued a statement saying in  part we continue to work closely with  the Pennsylvania Department of Health  and the Centers for Disease Control to  and Prevention to investigate and ensure  that proactive measures already taken  have eradicated this bacteria as well as  to prevent any additional cases  according to the CDC there are an  estimated 51,000 Pseudomonas infections  in the u.s. each year with roughly 400  deaths doctors say the bacterium should  not be a concern however for any mom  delivering a child after 32 weeks so  they're still looking for how this got  into the hospital oh no yeah that's the  most frightening of all not to know the  answer to that question and the  directing moms who may deliver early to  other regional hospitals which is a good  thing correct  Anna thank you very much
 

